  factId: fact_sun_coral_reef
  habitatName: Coral Reef Matrix
  category: Biogenic Aragonite Ecosystem
  zoneIndex: 0
  depthRangeText: "50\u2013100 m"
  habitatDescription: An expansive three-dimensional calcium carbonate fortress constructed
    over thousands of years by hermatypic stony corals (Scleractinia) and crustose
    coralline algae in sunlit waters.
  ecologicalSignificance: Known as the "rainforests of the sea," coral reefs support
    over 25% of all marine species while covering under 0.1% of the seafloor. They
    serve as vital natural breakwaters, attenuating up to 97% of coastal wave energy.
  interestingFact: Corals are animal colonies living in mutualism with photosynthetic
    microscopic algae (zooxanthellae). The algae produce up to 90% of the polyp's
    energy and provide corals their vibrant living coloration.
  targetBiome: 2
  citationSource: NOAA Coral Reef Conservation Program / Smithsonian Ocean Portal
